  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/40/Daredevil_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The FF needs a lawyer to look over the new lease to the Baxter Building, so the Thing calls on Nelson and Murdock.&nbsp; Foggy recommends Murdock, since he's more familiar with them, so Ben gets the information and heads out in the Fantasti-Car with the rest of the FF, who are on their way to Washington DC to receive a medal.\n\nMeanwhile, Daredevil is tracking down car thieves, which he finds in a garage, repainting them.&nbsp; At one point though, he uses a tire as a slingshot and picks up a car engine to hurl at an oncoming truck.&nbsp; (Come <i>on</i> now!&nbsp; He has time to actually pick up an engine?&nbsp; Not likely he could even <i>lift</i> it, much less stretch out a tire the way you see it done in that panel!&nbsp; And the picture shows it on top of his body too!&nbsp; <i>No way</i>!)&nbsp; But their boss turns out to be Electro (last seen in [LINK:1963:ASM_9|ASM #9|2027-03-13]), and he now says he's \"bored stealing cars anyway.\"&nbsp; He hears that the FF is out of town and decides to sneak into their HQ and steal some \"scientific secrets.\"\n\nWhen Matt returns to the office and is told about the FF job, Karen says she's been in contact with an eye doctor who thinks he might be able to cure Matt's blindness.&nbsp; Matt is not really interested though, as regaining his sight might come at the expense of losing his other enhanced senses.\n\nMatt approaches the Baxter Building and senses danger, but above, Electro has broken in, and once inside, he sees Daredevil on a monitor - he's entering as well!&nbsp; Now's his chance to get even with him for breaking up his car-stealing side business.&nbsp; Electro begins his attack, eventually overcoming DD and decides to put him in the FF's rocket and send it off into space, so it will look as if DD stole it.\n\nSoon, DD awakes to find he's bound inside the spacecraft, now orbiting the Earth!&nbsp; He frees himself and knows the craft is a <i>manned</i> rocket, complete with controls, so he finally lands it in Central Park.&nbsp; (Amazing that a lawyer could work out how to land a spacecraft.)&nbsp; He hijacks a horse and has it jump over cars through town until he's finally back at the Baxter Building.\n\nWorried that the electronic alarms will alert Electro that he's coming, he hops a helicopter and waits for it to fly above the building.&nbsp; He crashes through a skylight just as Electro manages to break into a vault containing mathematical equations that he's sure he can sell!&nbsp; Electro runs for it, trying to lose DD in traffic once on the ground, but his unique electric emanations make it easy for DD to stay zeroed in on him.&nbsp; Finally, he enters a movie theater, with DD in hot pursuit.\n\nDD finally manages to ensnare him in one of the large theater curtains however.&nbsp; The police surround him, spraying him with water to keep him short-circuited.&nbsp; DD returns to the Baxter Building and repairs the vault Electro destroyed.\n\nJust as he returns to the office, the FF burst in, wanting to sue someone for breaking their skylight.&nbsp; But they're disappointed he hasn't had time to get to their original request and say they'll just find \"some other lawyers...who work a bit faster!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "Write something",
  "subtitle": "Write something",
  "review_text": "let's face it - after the <i>brilliant</i> first issue, there wasn't much question the second issue would be a comparative let-down, and this one certainly was.&nbsp; The guest appearances by both the Thing (w/ the FF) and Electro make it <i>somewhat</i> more interesting and helped elevate it to a mere \"readable\" status, but the story itself was...well, let's just say it was nothing to write home about.\n\nDaredevil gets shot up into space, orbits the Earth, is somehow able to land in Central Park and gets back to the Baxter Building to confront Electro, who somehow is still there.&nbsp; Hey, I'm sure it takes a bit of time for a spacecraft to orbit the Earth, and I don't imagine Electro took his time, perhaps raiding the FF's kitchen, helping himself to snacks while he was there.&nbsp; He would have broken in, done the deed and left, so the timing doesn't work for me.\n\nAnd I really can't excuse the whole \"I think I'll slingshot a car engine with this rubber tire because I've detected a truck barreling toward me\" early in the story.&nbsp; That defies belief - DD has the strength of an ordinary human, and I've never met anyone who could lift a car engine, much less stretch an automotive tire like that!&nbsp; And furthermore, it would have made a lot more sense to acrobatically leap out of the way, perhaps clobbering the driver through the window with something, resulting in the same crash.&nbsp; <i>Come on man</i>!<i></i>\n\nThese things really spoiled the story.&nbsp; But on the positive side, the little thought bubble explanations of how he's sensing his environment are good.&nbsp; They do help reinforce that he's \"seeing\" things differently than the rest of us do.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI think the readable VG grade is generous.&nbsp; DD is still intriguing, but the mechanics of this story were not good, and the relatively lame ending in the movie theater disappointed as well.&nbsp; Electro snagged by a curtain?&nbsp; <i>Nah</i>!\n\nThe art is done by Joe Orlando and Vince Colletta, both of whom were veteran comics creators by this time, but this is the first time I've seen their names in any Marvel superhero-related work.&nbsp; Vinnie Colletta in particular would go on to become a well-known inker, and despite the criticism hurled his way (some of it deserved), I always particularly enjoyed the way he did women's eyes - they were much more lush than anyone else's - though that does not show as well in this particular issue.",
  "highlight": "Write something"
}